<h1>Lambda Configuration Parameters</h1>
<p>The tutorial suite's <strong>Advanced Configuration Parameters</strong> group contains the Lambda Selector and
	Lambda Multi-Selector tests covered in this chapter. Their <strong>Lambda Configuration Parameters</strong>
	obtain selectable values from callbacks at run-configuration time. Unlike the fixed choices introduced in the
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/basic-configuration-parameters-group/">Basic Configuration Parameters
	group</a>, these fields can retrieve current options from external or changing sources. This chapter covers
	single-value and multi-value Lambda selectors.</p>

<h2 id="lambda-selector-parameter">Lambda Selector Parameter</h2>
<p>Earlier Maze tests require a manually downloaded application and the folder containing its executable. This
	test replaces that setup with a version selector. Selecting a Maze app release from GitHub downloads the
	matching binary in the background or reuses its cached copy.</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/lambda-selector-parameter-test.png", "Lambda Selector Parameter test selected in the tutorial suite, showing an empty Maze App Version field with its selection button", "large") }}

<h3 id="selecting-a-version">Selecting a value</h3>
<p>Select the button at the right of <strong>Maze App Version</strong> to open its dialog. Leave the filter empty
	to request every available release, or enter part of a tag such as <code>v3</code>, then select
	<strong>Fetch</strong>. The returned release tags populate the drop-down:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/selection-drop-box.png", "Maze App Version selection dialog after fetching releases, with Maze-v3.0.0, Maze-v2.0.0 and Maze-v1.0.0 shown in the open drop-down", "large") }}
<p>Choose one version and select <strong>OK</strong> to assign its tag to the test configuration:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/selected-version.png", "Lambda Selector Parameter configuration after confirming the selection, with maze-v3.0.0 assigned to Maze App Version", "large") }}
<p>Fetching again replaces the available choices with the callback's latest result while preserving the current
	selection when that value is still available.</p>

<h3 id="config-field">Defining a dynamic selector</h3>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_lambda_selector_parameter/") }}

<p>A regular <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#selection-types"><code>OrcraftConfigField.Select</code></a>
	receives its choices through <code>options</code> when the configuration class is defined.
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#selection-types"><code>OrcraftConfigField.SelectLambda</code></a>
	instead receives an <code>options_lambda</code> callback that the controller invokes when options are requested. This mechanism
	supports values obtained from external or changing sources.</p>

<p>The test developer declares a <code>SelectLambda</code> field in the test's configuration class and assigns
	its provider callback through <code>options_lambda</code>:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('maze_app_version = OrcraftConfigField.SelectLambda(
	default="",
	label="Maze App Version",
	input_placeholder="Filter versions (e.g. v3)",
	button_text="Fetch",
	options_lambda=lambda text: fetch_maze_app_release_versions(text),
	layout=OrcraftConfigField.Layout.NewRow,
)') }}
<p>The field starts empty. <code>input_placeholder</code> describes the optional filter accepted by the callback,
  while <code>button_text</code> labels the action that invokes it - both are listed with the other
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#options">common config field options</a>. The lambda receives the text entered in the
  dialog and returns the values supplied by <code>fetch_maze_app_release_versions</code>.</p>

<h3 id="versions-provider">Supplying dynamic options</h3>
<p>The test developer implements the option-provider function separately from the configuration field. The
	function may read from any source appropriate to the test. In this tutorial, the provider calls a shared
	GitHub helper that requests the Maze app releases, excludes drafts, and returns the release tags as a tuple.
	When filter text is present, the provider returns only tags containing that text:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('def fetch_github_release_versions(owner: str, repo: str, filter_text: str = "") -> tuple[str, ...]:
	url = f"https://api.github.com/repos/{owner}/{repo}/releases"
	request = Request(url, headers=_gh_headers())
	try:
		with urlopen(request, timeout=8) as response:
			releases = json.loads(response.read().decode("utf-8"))
	except Exception:
		return tuple()

	versions = [release["tag_name"] for release in releases if not release.get("draft")]
	query = filter_text.strip().lower()
	if query:
		versions = [version for version in versions if query in version.lower()]
	return tuple(versions)


def fetch_maze_app_release_versions(filter_text: str = "") -> tuple[str, ...]:
	return fetch_github_release_versions(MAZE_APP_OWNER, MAZE_APP_REPO, filter_text)') }}
<p>The constants passed by the wrapper identify the <code>GMCoreAI/orcraft-maze-app</code> repository. Keeping
	the repository-specific wrapper separate allows the generic helper to serve other GitHub-backed selectors.</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/common/github_repo_access.py") }}

<h3 id="using-the-selected-version">Reading the selected value</h3>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	maze_app = MazeApp.from_release(
		self.config.maze_app_version,
		self.config.command,
		force=self.config.force_redownload,
	)

	maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>At run time, the selected release tag is passed to <code>MazeApp.from_release</code>. The helper downloads the
  matching binary for the current platform, or reuses its cached copy unless
  <strong>Re-download (ignore cached binary)</strong> is enabled, then runs the selected command.</p>

<h2 id="lambda-multi-selector-parameter">Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter</h2>
<p><strong>Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter</strong> follows the same principle but supports several values from
	the callback. This test selects multiple Maze app releases and runs the same command against every selected
	version.</p>

<h3 id="selecting-multiple-versions">Selecting multiple values</h3>
<p>Open <strong>Maze App Versions</strong> and select <strong>Fetch</strong>, as in the single-value selector. The
	same callback returns the available release tags, while this dialog displays a checkbox beside each option.
	Select each version to include, then select <strong>OK</strong>:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/lambda-multi-selector-parameter.png", "Maze App Versions dialog for Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter, with Maze-v3.0.0 and Maze-v1.0.0 selected and Maze-v2.0.0 unselected", "large") }}
<p>The selected tags are stored together in the <strong>Maze App Versions</strong> field:</p>
{{ media.zoomableImage("/assets/images/screenshots/tutorial/lambda-configuration-parameters/selected-versions.png", "Lambda Multi-Selector Parameter configuration with maze-v3.0.0 and maze-v1.0.0 assigned to Maze App Versions", "large") }}
<p>When the test runs, each selected release is downloaded in the background or loaded from the cache. This
	workflow eliminates manual binary downloads and folder-list configuration.</p>

<h3 id="defining-a-dynamic-multi-selector">Defining a dynamic multi-selector</h3>
<p>The implementation differs from the single-value selector in two respects: the configuration uses
	<a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/#selection-types"><code>MultiSelectLambda</code></a> with a tuple
	default, and the test iterates over the selected values.</p>
<p>Source directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyPath("orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_advanced_configuration_parameters/test_lambda_multi_selector_parameter/") }}

{{ codeblock.block('maze_app_versions = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectLambda(
	default=(),
	label="Maze App Versions",
	input_placeholder="Filter versions (e.g. v3)",
	button_text="Fetch",
	options_lambda=lambda text: fetch_maze_app_release_versions(text),
	required=True,
	layout=OrcraftConfigField.Layout.NewRow,
)') }}
<p><code>required=True</code> prevents the test from running until at least one version is selected. The field
	reuses the same <code>fetch_maze_app_release_versions</code> provider shown in
	<a class="text-accent" href="#versions-provider">Supplying dynamic options</a>. The callback only supplies
  the available choices; the field type determines whether the user can select one value or several.</p>

<h3 id="reading-multiple-selected-values">Reading multiple selected values</h3>
{{ codeblock.block('def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):

	for tag in self.config.maze_app_versions:

		maze_app = MazeApp.from_release(
			tag,
			self.config.command,
			force=self.config.force_redownload,
		)

		maze_app.run()

	return case.result') }}
<p>The configuration value is a tuple of selected tags. <code>_run_case</code> loops over it, resolves each
  release through <code>MazeApp.from_release</code>, and runs the configured command once per version.</p>

<p>After defining callback-driven configuration choices, continue to
	<a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/matrix-and-cases/">Matrix and Cases</a> to generate test cases from
	combinations of configuration values and access their dimension values at run time.</p>